#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[In] these words the apostle describes the state of mind of the Christians he was writing to, under the persecutions they were then suffering. These persecutions are what he has in view in the two verses before, when he speaks of #emph[the trial of their faith,] and of their #emph[suffering grief in various trials.]

Such trials benefit true religion in three ways. First, by them its truth is shown, and it appears to be indeed #emph[true religion.] Trials, above all other things, tend to distinguish between true religion and false, and to make the difference between them plainly appear. That is why they are called by the name of #emph[trials] in the verse just before the text, and in countless other places. They try the faith and religion of those who profess religion, to show what sort it is, as apparent gold is tried in the fire and shown to be true gold or not. And the faith of true Christians, tried in this way and proved to be true, is #emph[found to praise, and honor, and glory,] as that verse before the text says.

Then, these trials benefit true religion further: they not only show its #emph[truth,] but they make its genuine #emph[beauty] and #emph[loveliness] remarkably appear. True virtue never appears so lovely as when it is most oppressed, and the divine excellence of real Christianity is never displayed to such advantage as when it is under the greatest trials. It is then that true faith appears much more precious than gold, and on this account it is #emph[found to praise, and honor, and glory.]

And again, another benefit such trials bring to true religion is that they purify it and increase it. They not only show it to be true, but also tend to refine it and free it from the mixtures of what is false, which encumber and hinder it, so that nothing may be left but what is true. They tend to make the loveliness of true religion appear to the best advantage, as was observed before. And not only that: they tend to increase its beauty, by establishing and confirming it, making it more lively and vigorous, and purifying it from the things that obscured its luster and glory. As gold that is tried in the fire is purged of its alloy and all remaining dross, and comes out more solid and beautiful, so true faith, tried as gold is tried in the fire, becomes more precious. And so it too is #emph[found to praise, and honor, and glory.] The apostle seems to have in view each of these benefits that persecutions bring to true religion, in the verse before the text.

And in the text the apostle observes how true religion worked in the Christians he was writing to, under their persecutions, so that these benefits of persecution appeared in them. That is, he observes what kind of working of true religion it was in them by which their religion, under persecution, was shown to be #emph[true] religion; by which it eminently appeared in the genuine #emph[beauty] and #emph[loveliness] of true religion; and by which it also appeared to be #emph[increased] and #emph[purified,] and so was likely to be #emph[found to praise, and honor, and glory at the appearing of Jesus Christ.] And there were two kinds of working, or exercise, of true religion in them under their sufferings that the apostle takes notice of in the text, in which these benefits appeared.

1\. #emph[Love to Christ:] “Though you#super[+] have not seen Him, you#super[+] love Him.” The world was ready to wonder what strange principle it was that moved them to expose themselves to such great sufferings: to forsake the things that were seen, and renounce all that was dear and pleasant, all that was the object of sense. To the men of the world around them they seemed to be out of their minds, and to act as though they hated themselves. There was nothing in their view that could lead them to suffer like this, or support them under such trials and carry them through. But although there was nothing seen that influenced and supported them like this, nothing the world saw, or that the Christians themselves ever saw with their bodily eyes, they had a supernatural principle of love to something #emph[unseen.] They loved Jesus Christ, for they saw him spiritually, though the world did not see him, and though they themselves had never seen him with bodily eyes.

2\. #emph[Joy in Christ.] Though their outward sufferings were very grievous, their inward spiritual joys were greater than their sufferings. These joys supported them and enabled them to suffer with cheerfulness.

There are two things the apostle takes notice of in the text concerning this joy. 1. The way in which it arises, the way in which Christ, though unseen, is its foundation, namely, by faith, which is the evidence of things not seen: “Though you#super[+] do not see Him now, you#super[+] #emph[believe] in Him and rejoice.” 2. The nature of this joy: “unspeakable and full of glory.” It is #emph[unspeakable] in its #emph[kind:] very different from worldly joys and carnal delights, of a far purer, more sublime and heavenly nature, being something supernatural and truly divine, and so excellent beyond all expression. There were no words to set forth its sublimity and its exquisite sweetness. It was unspeakable also in #emph[degree,] for it pleased God to give them this holy joy with a liberal hand and in large measure in their state of persecution.

Their joy was #emph[full of glory.] Although the joy was unspeakable, and no words were sufficient to describe it, something might still be said of it, and no words were more fit to represent its excellence than these: that it was #emph[full of glory,] or, as the original has it, #emph[glorified joy.] In rejoicing with this joy their minds were filled, as it were, with a glorious brightness, and their natures were exalted and perfected. It was a most worthy and noble rejoicing, which did not corrupt and debase the mind, as many carnal joys do, but greatly beautified and dignified it. It was a foretaste of the joy of heaven, which raised their minds to a degree of heavenly blessedness. It filled their minds with the light of God’s glory, and made them shine themselves with some share of that glory.

So the proposition, or doctrine, that I would draw from these words is this:

DOCT. #emph[True religion, in great Part, consists in holy affections.]

The apostle observes and remarks on the workings and exercises of religion in the Christians he was writing to, in which their religion appeared to be true and of the right kind. This was when it had its greatest trial of what sort it was, being tried by persecution as gold is tried in the fire. It was when their religion not only proved true but was at its purest, cleansed of its dross and of the mixtures of what was not true, and when religion appeared in them most in its genuine excellence and native beauty and was found to praise, and honor, and glory. And we see that he singles out the religious affections of #emph[love] and #emph[joy] that were then in exercise in them. These are the exercises of religion he takes notice of, in which their religion appeared in this way, true and pure and in its proper glory.

Here I would:

I. Show what is meant by the #emph[affections.]

II. Observe some things that make it evident that a great part of true religion lies in the affections.
